  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 302 AM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nPrecipitation/Storm Trends...Isolated showers prevailed across the \nregion early this morning along an 850mb warm front/low-level WAA \ngradient gradually lifting northeast across the region. Anticipate \nthis shower activity to continue into the afternoon, mainly across \nthe Northwoods. A few rumbles of thunder are possible within this \nshower activity, mainly during the afternoon when a couple hundred \nJ/kg of CAPE will be present. Meanwhile, a weak surface front will \nslowly lift into central and east-central WI throughout the day. \nJust like with any warm front, CAMs are all over the place with if \nconvection will develop along this warm front. If any convection \ndevelops along the front, central and east-central WI are the most \nlikely areas to see any shower/elevated storm activity. The surface \nwarm front will then lift north over the area tonight into early \nMonday, allowing for additional shower/elevated storm activity to \ncontinue overnight across the entire forecast area. Cannot rule out \nthe potential for a strong elevated storm capable of producing gusty \nwinds with MUCAPE between 1000-2000 J/kg and effective shear around \n30 kts during the evening and overnight. These storms would be also \nproduce brief heavy downpours with PWATs around 2 inches during this \ntime.\n\nAfter the shower/storm activity ends Monday morning, much of the day \nshould remain dry with weak capping in place. However, by late \nMonday afternoon/evening, a surface low will track across northern \nWI/Upper MI, which may spark some convection along its trailing weak \ncold front in central and north-central WI if there's enough forcing \nto break the weak cap. Models are not in agreement with this \nconvection developing, likely due to the capping. But will continue \nto monitor this for severe weather potential as MUCAPE would be \naround 1500-3000 J/kg and deep-layer shear of 30-40 kts.\n\nDry conditions return for Tuesday as the aforementioned cold front \nweakens and sags south over southern WI. A couple shortwaves then \nlook to move over the region through the remainder of the work week, \nbringing chances for showers and storms at times.\n\nTemperatures/Humidity...As the warm front lifts north today, temps \nwill climb above normal for central and east-central WI into the \nupper 70s to mid 80s, while temps will top out in the mid 70s across \nthe north due to the precip chances. Temperatures will be even warm \non Monday and Tuesday with highs in the mid to upper 80s, with a few \nspots topping out around 90. The persistent southerly flow will \nusher in humid conditions today and Monday as well, with dew points \nincreasing into the upper 60s and low 70s. Depending on cloud cover \nand precip chances, heat indices on Monday may rise into the low to \nmid 90s.",
